---
// Sources and licences of the Dante Reader (src/pages/sources.astro).
import { LEMMA_FIXES, MARKS, PLACEMENTS, VERSE_FIXES } from "./alignment";
import { NO_TOYNBEE, TOYNBEE_HEADS } from "./names";
import { FILES, KAIKKI, UD_COMMIT } from "./sources";

const fixes = Object.entries(LEMMA_FIXES);
const placements = Object.keys(PLACEMENTS).length + Object.keys(VERSE_FIXES).length;
const marks = Object.keys(MARKS).length;
const respelt = Object.keys(TOYNBEE_HEADS).length;
const unlisted = Object.keys(NO_TOYNBEE).length;
---
<h2>Italian</h2>
<dl>
  <dt>Dante Alighieri, <i>La Commedia secondo l’antica vulgata</i>, ed. Giorgio Petrocchi (Milan: Mondadori, 1966–67; 2nd ed. Florence: Le Lettere, 1994)</dt>
  <dd>
    Petrocchi’s critical text, the standard edition of the Commedia, as the DanteSearch corpus of the University of
    Pisa gives it and UD_Italian-Old carries it. Its spelling is Petrocchi’s (<i>ogne</i>, <i>sanza</i>,
    <i>vegna</i>); its punctuation follows the treebank, which drops the diaeresis Petrocchi prints for metre
    (<i>süave</i> appears as <i>suave</i>).
  </dd>
  <dt>UD_Italian-Old: lemmas and morphology</dt>
  <dd>
    Every word’s lemma and morphology come from
    the <a href="https://github.com/UniversalDependencies/UD_Italian-Old">UD_Italian-Old</a> treebank (Claudia
    Corbetta, Marco Passarotti, Flavio Massimiliano Cecchini and Giovanni Moretti, Università Cattolica del Sacro
    Cuore, Milan), as released in Universal Dependencies 2.18, commit <code>{UD_COMMIT}</code>,
    licensed <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/">CC BY-SA 4.0</a>. It converts the lemmas and
    grammatical analysis of DanteSearch (Mirko Tavoni and others, University of Pisa) to Universal Dependencies,
    with corrections, and adds dependency relations, which the reader does not show. Lemmas are modern Italian
    citation forms: <i>loco</i> is filed under <i>luogo</i>, <i>fé</i> under <i>fare</i> or <i>fede</i>. A
    written word that joins two words (<i>del</i> = <i>di</i> + <i>il</i>, <i>dimmi</i> = <i>di’</i> + <i>mi</i>,
    <i>meco</i> = <i>me</i> + <i>con</i>) shows the analysis of each. The treebank’s README calls it still under
    revision; the reader is pinned to one release.
    <details>
      <summary>Lemmas corrected ({fixes.length})</summary>
      <p>The treebank writes a few final accents as apostrophes and misspells one lemma. Every token is changed.</p>
      <ul>
        {fixes.map(([from, to]) => <li><i lang="it">{from}</i> becomes <i lang="it">{to}</i></li>)}
      </ul>
    </details>
  </dd>
  <dt>Morphology</dt>
  <dd>
    The treebank’s UD features are shown as Italian grammar names them: <i>Tense=Past</i> with imperfective aspect
    is the imperfect, with perfective aspect the past historic (<i>passato remoto</i>). The treebank marks most past
    participles passive, including those of compound tenses, so the reader does not show their voice. The eight
    lines of Arnaut Daniel in Purgatorio 26 are Old Occitan; the treebank analyses them as Italian words and labels
    them Old French, and the reader labels them Old Occitan. Latin words (<i>Beati pauperes spiritu</i>) and
    words of no language (<i>Pape Satàn, pape Satàn aleppe</i>) are marked as words of another language.
  </dd>
  <dt>Lines and spacing</dt>
  <dd>
    The treebank gives the canto and line of nearly every word. It leaves them off punctuation and off 1,250 words
    of the Paradiso. Those words are placed by comparing the treebank’s words, in order, with the lines of the
    Italian text of Project Gutenberg #1012 (<a href={FILES["gutenberg-italian.txt"]![0]}>pinned by hash</a>), which has
    the same cantos and lines and a few dozen other readings; punctuation goes with the word before it, opening
    quotation marks with the word after. Where the two texts disagree on a line, or Gutenberg reads a word otherwise
    and its neighbours fall on different lines, an entry in the build’s tables decides ({placements} entries).
    The treebank records spaces after words completely only for the Inferno; the reader spaces the whole poem by
    the rules that reproduce the Inferno’s spacing: none before closing punctuation, none after an opening mark, and
    none after an elided word ending in a consonant (<i>ch’i’</i>, <i>l’altre</i>) except <i>ver’</i>. The
    Purgatorio and Paradiso write quotation marks as straight quotes, which open and close in turn; the Purgatorio
    writes some quotations within speeches as » … «. Such marks are printed as curly quotes, and {marks} marks that
    the rules would misplace are listed in the build.
  </dd>
</dl>

<h2>English</h2>
<dl>
  <dt>Henry Wadsworth Longfellow, <i>The Divine Comedy of Dante Alighieri</i> (Boston: Ticknor and Fields, 1867)</dt>
  <dd>
    Public domain. Project Gutenberg #1004 (<a href={FILES["longfellow.txt"]![0]}>pinned by hash</a>), produced by
    Dennis McCarthy. Longfellow translated line for line in blank verse, so every canto has the Italian’s number of
    lines and its tercets. Each row of the reader is one tercet, and line <i>n</i> of the English is line <i>n</i> of
    the Italian, though the sense sometimes runs across lines differently. His one note, the translation of Arnaut
    Daniel’s Occitan in Purgatorio 26, is shown as a note to that tercet. The subject of each canto under its title
    is the first item of the edition’s table of contents.
  </dd>
</dl>

<h2>Dictionaries</h2>
<dl>
  <dt>Wiktionary, Italian entries</dt>
  <dd>
    English Wiktionary’s Italian entries, extracted by <a href="https://github.com/tatuylonen/wiktextract">Wiktextract</a>
    and published by <a href="https://kaikki.org/dictionary/Italian/">kaikki.org</a>,
    licensed <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/">CC BY-SA 4.0</a> and the GNU Free Documentation
    License; © Wiktionary contributors. kaikki.org replaces the file weekly, so the reader uses the Internet Archive’s
    capture of 21 June 2025 (<code>{KAIKKI[0]}</code>, SHA-256 <code>{KAIKKI[1]}</code>). A lemma finds the entries spelt
    as it is, else those spelt with other accents; a verb Wiktionary lists only as pronominal is found under
    its <i>-rsi</i> form (<i>accorgere</i>, <i>accorgersi</i>). Wiktionary records many of Dante’s words as archaic or
    poetic spellings of a modern word (<i>surgere</i>, an alternative form of <i>sorgere</i>); the panel then shows
    that entry and the modern word’s. Sense labels (<i>archaic</i>, <i>poetic</i>, <i>figuratively</i>) are
    Wiktionary’s. About one word in a hundred has a lemma Wiktionary lacks, mostly Latinisms and rare old forms.
  </dd>
  <dt>Paget Toynbee, <i>A Concise Dictionary of Proper Names and Notable Matters in the Works of Dante</i> (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1914)</dt>
  <dd>
    Public domain. The Internet Archive’s OCR text of the University of California’s
    copy (<a href={FILES["toynbee.txt"]![0]}>pinned by hash</a>), cut into entries and repaired by the build: page
    headings removed, words divided at line ends joined, misread digits in citations corrected against the lines they
    cite. Citations of the Commedia are written as the reader cites (<i>Inf. 5.116</i>); citations of Dante’s other
    works are left as printed. Every name in the poem is matched to its entry: by spelling, or by a table where
    Toynbee spells the name otherwise than the treebank’s lemma ({respelt} names). {unlisted} lemmas the treebank
    files as names have no entry, mostly words it capitalizes for personification or reverence. Where Toynbee has
    several entries under one name, the panel shows those that cite the canto being read.
  </dd>
</dl>
